'''Tachibara Prefecture''' ({{lang|ja|立原県}}, ''Tachibara-ken'') is a prefecture of [[Okaiken]] located in the southern interior of the island of [[Ishijima]], stretching from the [[Katsuran Sea]] coast in the west to the mountainous interior that borders [[Shimabara Prefecture|Shimabara]], [[Kiso Prefecture|Kiso]], and [[Ehime Prefecture|Ehime]]. Its population as of 2026 was 921,920, with approximately 37% concentrated in the prefectural capital of [[Tachibara, Tachibara|Tachibara]] (pop. 368,701). Tachibara is a rural prefecture defined by its food culture—its [[udon]] noodles, [[soy sauce]], and [[indigo dye]] are among the most recognised artisanal products in Okaiken.

Tachibara's identity is inseparable from its three great traditions: {{nihongo|'''Tachibara udon'''|立原うどん|Tachibara-udon}}, the thick, chewy wheat noodles that are the prefecture's most famous export; {{nihongo|'''Tachibara shōyu'''|立原醤油|Tachibara-shōyu}}, the dark, complex soy sauce that is aged in the region's warm, humid climate; and {{nihongo|'''Tachibara ai'''|立原藍|Tachibara-ai}}, the deep blue indigo dye that has been produced in the region's fermentation vats for over a millennium. These three traditions share a common foundation—fermentation—and a common environment: the warm, wet, mountain-ringed valleys of the Tachibara interior, where the constant humidity and the pure mountain water create ideal conditions for the slow, patient processes that produce the prefecture's distinctive flavours and colours.

== History ==

=== Ancient and pre-shogunate period ===
The Tachibara interior was originally settled by [[Atsu]] agricultural communities who cultivated wheat, soybeans, and the indigo plant in the fertile valleys of the interior highlands. The warm, humid climate and the abundant mountain water made the region ideal for fermentation-based food production, and the Atsu communities developed the earliest forms of the soy sauce brewing and indigo dyeing traditions that would later become the prefecture's signature industries.

The [[Kajika]] expansion brought wheat cultivation techniques from the Shendan islands, and the combination of wheat and the Atsu fermentation tradition produced the first {{nihongo|'''Tachibara udon'''|立原うどん}}—thick, chewy noodles made from wheat flour, mountain water, and salt, which were originally a ceremonial food served at Shinto festivals. The noodles' distinctive texture—firm, elastic, and slightly translucent—was the result of the region's hard water and the traditional {{nihongo|"foot-kneading"|足踏み|ashibumi}} technique, in which the dough is kneaded by stepping on it with the feet to develop the gluten structure.

=== The Honda Shogunate and the Tachibara Domain ===
The [[Tachibara Domain]] ({{lang|ja|立原藩}}, ''Tachibara-han'') was established under the [[Honda Shogunate]] as a retainer territory of the [[Kawakami clan]], hereditary vassals of the Honda who controlled the southern approaches to the Ishijima interior. The domain's wealth was built on its three fermentation traditions—udon, soy sauce, and indigo dye—all of which were produced in quantities that exceeded the domain's needs and were traded throughout Ishijima.

The Shogunate period saw the development of the {{nihongo|'''Tachibara Shōyu Breweries'''|立原醤油蔵|Tachibara-shōyu-gura}}, a cluster of soy sauce breweries in the [[Kawakami, Tachibara|Kawakami]] valley that used the region's warm, humid climate to produce a naturally fermented soy sauce with a deeper, more complex flavour than the faster-brewed products of the coast. The [[Kawakami Shōyu]], the oldest continuously operating soy sauce brewery in the prefecture, was founded in 1486 and remains one of the most respected producers in Okaiken.

The indigo dyeing tradition also flourished during the shogunate. The {{nihongo|'''Tachibara ai'''|立原藍}} dye, produced by fermenting the leaves of the indigo plant in a {{nihongo|"sukumo"|蒅}} vat for 100–120 days, produces a deep, luminous blue that was used for the formal garments of the shogunal aristocracy. The dye's intensity and its resistance to fading made it the preferred colour for ceremonial clothing, and the Tachibara indigo was traded throughout the Shendan as a luxury product.

=== Modern era ===
The [[Saito Restoration]] of 1871 converted the Tachibara Domain into Tachibara Prefecture. The modernisation of the Meiji period saw the udon, soy sauce, and indigo industries reorganised for the national market, and the [[Tachibara Udon Cooperative]], established in 1928, standardised production while preserving the traditional hand-stretched technique.

The post-[[Great Adonian War]] period saw Tachibara udon achieve national fame. The [[Tachibara Udon Road]] ({{lang|ja|立原うどん街道}}, ''Tachibara-udon kaidō''), a 35-kilometre route connecting the udon workshops of the interior valleys, became a major domestic food tourism destination, and the prefecture's udon restaurants are now among the most celebrated in Okaiken. The [[Tachibara Indigo Revival]], a movement that began in the 1990s to preserve the traditional dyeing technique against competition from synthetic dyes, has been remarkably successful, and the Tachibara ai is now exported as a premium artisanal product to Fosian fashion houses.

== Geography ==

Tachibara Prefecture occupies the southern interior of Ishijima, stretching from the [[Katsuran Sea]] coast in the west to the mountainous interior that borders [[Shimabara Prefecture|Shimabara]], [[Kiso Prefecture|Kiso]], and [[Ehime Prefecture|Ehime]]. The prefecture's geography is defined by the transition from the narrow Katsuran Sea coastal plain through the central Tachibara Hills to the elevated interior valleys that support the prefecture's wheat, soybean, and indigo farms.

The {{nihongo|'''Tachibara Hills'''|立原丘陵|Tachibara-kyūryō}}, a range of low, rounded ridges rising to 200–400 m, form the central spine of the prefecture. The hills are deeply incised by river valleys that drain westward into the Katsuran Sea, creating a series of sheltered, humid valleys that are ideal for fermentation-based food production. The [[Tachibara River]] ({{lang|ja|立原川}}, ''Tachibara-gawa''), the prefecture's longest waterway at 58 km, flows from the interior highlands through the Tachibara Basin to the coast.

The [[Oshika Highlands]] ({{lang|ja|大鹿高原}}, ''Oshika-kōgen''), in the southeastern reaches of the prefecture, rise to over 900 m and represent the transition zone between the Tachibara Hills and the Shimabara Volcanic Group. The highlands are cooler and wetter than the coast, supporting wheat and soybean cultivation.

