== Economy ==

Tachibara Prefecture's economy is built on three fermentation-based traditions: udon noodles, soy sauce brewing, and indigo dyeing. The warm, humid climate and the pure mountain water of the interior valleys create conditions that are uniquely suited to the slow, patient processes that produce these artisanal products, and the prefecture has leveraged its food culture into a distinctive economic profile that combines premium agriculture with heritage tourism.

=== Tachibara udon ===
{{nihongo|'''Tachibara udon'''|立原うどん|Tachibara-udon}} is the prefecture's most famous product and one of the most recognised regional foods in Okaiken. The noodles are made from wheat flour, mountain water, and salt, using the traditional {{nihongo|"foot-kneading"|足踏み|ashibumi}} technique in which the dough is kneaded by stepping on it with the feet to develop the gluten structure. The resulting noodles are thick, firm, and slightly translucent, with a distinctive chewiness that is markedly different from the thinner, softer udon of other regions.

The [[Tachibara Udon Cooperative]], based in [[Kawakami, Tachibara|Kawakami]], coordinates production for the approximately 180 udon workshops that operate in the interior valleys. The [[Tachibara Udon Road]] ({{lang|ja|立原うどん街道}}, ''Tachibara-udon kaidō''), a 35-kilometre route connecting the workshops, draws over 600,000 visitors annually and is one of the most popular food tourism destinations in Okaiken. The [[Tachibara Udon Festival]], held in February during the dry season, features noodle-making competitions, tasting events, and the selection of the "Golden Bowl" — the finest bowl of udon of the year.

=== Soy sauce brewing ===
The {{nihongo|'''Tachibara shōyu'''|立原醤油|Tachibara-shōyu}} is the prefecture's second most important product and one of the most respected soy sauces in Okaiken. The soy sauce is brewed using a traditional natural fermentation process that takes 18–24 months—far longer than the 6-month industrial process used by the major national brands. The warm, humid climate of the Tachibara interior allows the fermentation to proceed slowly and evenly, producing a soy sauce with a deeper, more complex flavour and a richer, darker colour than the faster-brewed products.

The [[Kawakami Shōyu]], founded in 1486, is the oldest continuously operating soy sauce brewery in the prefecture and one of the most respected in Okaiken. The brewery's flagship product, {{nihongo|"Kawakami Junmai Shōyu"|川上純米醤油}}, is a premium naturally brewed soy sauce that is aged for 24 months in cedar vats and is sold at prices 5–10 times higher than the standard national brands. The [[Tachibara Shōyu Brewers' Association]], based in [[Kawakami, Tachibara|Kawakami]], maintains production standards for the approximately 45 soy sauce breweries that operate in the traditional style.

=== Indigo dyeing ===
The {{nihongo|'''Tachibara ai'''|立原藍|Tachibara-ai}} is the prefecture's third great tradition and one of the most distinctive artisanal products in Okaiken. The indigo dye is produced by fermenting the leaves of the indigo plant (''Persicaria tinctoria'') in a {{nihongo|"sukumo"|蒅}} vat for 100–120 days, producing a deep, luminous blue that is resistant to fading and has a distinctive depth and warmth that synthetic dyes cannot replicate.

The [[Tachibara Indigo Revival]], a movement that began in the 1990s to preserve the traditional dyeing technique against competition from synthetic dyes, has been remarkably successful. The [[Tachibara Indigo Cooperative]], based in [[Oshika, Tachibara|Oshika]], now coordinates production for approximately 25 active dye workshops, and the Tachibara ai is exported as a premium artisanal product to Fosian fashion houses and textile designers. The [[Tachibara Indigo Festival]], held in July during the indigo harvest, features dyeing demonstrations, fashion shows, and the ceremonial "Vat Blessing" at the oldest fermentation vat in the prefecture.
Tachibara operates as the traditional fermentation and natural dye heartland of the archipelago. Agricultural cooperatives cultivate broad-leaf polygonum indigo, fermenting harvested leaves in clay composting beds for one hundred days to yield concentrated sukumo dye cakes. In subterranean dye vats, master dyers ferment this sukumo with wood ash and wheat bran to produce Sanese Blue textiles, renowned for their natural antibacterial and fire-resistant qualities. The prefecture's food sector is anchored by century-old shōyu and miso breweries that ferment whole soybeans in open wooden vats, producing unpasteurized condiments of exceptional depth.

== Culture ==

=== Cuisine ===
Tachibara Prefecture's cuisine is defined by its udon and soy sauce. The signature dish is {{nihongo|'''Tachibara udon'''|立原うどん}}, served in a hot broth made from the prefecture's own soy sauce, ''katsuo'' dashi, and mountain water, topped with [[Ehime Prefecture|Ehime]] kabosu, scallions, and a raw egg. The dish is deceptively simple—the quality of the ingredients, particularly the noodles and the soy sauce, makes each bowl a distinctive experience.

Other local specialties include ''udon chazuke'' (udon noodles with green tea poured over, served with pickled vegetables and wasabi), ''Kawakami shōyu ramen'' (a soy sauce-based ramen broth that is considered one of the finest in Okaiken), and ''ai-zome tempura'' (tempura battered with a touch of indigo plant powder, producing a pale blue-green colour—more of a novelty than a staple, but a popular item at the Indigo Festival).

=== The Fermentation Trail ===
The {{nihongo|'''Tachibara Fermentation Trail'''|立原発酵街道|Tachibara-hakkō kaidō}} is a 42-kilometre walking route that connects the udon workshops, soy sauce breweries, and indigo dye studios of the interior valleys. The trail is the prefecture's most important heritage tourism product, drawing approximately 180,000 visitors annually, and is designed to showcase the shared fermentation heritage that connects the three traditions. The trail includes guided tastings, hands-on workshops, and overnight stays at traditional ''machiya'' townhouses that have been converted into guesthouses.

== Transportation ==

=== Rail ===
The [[Tachibara Line]] connects [[Tachibara Station]] to the [[Ishijima Shinkansen]] network at [[Ehime Station]], providing high-speed rail access to [[Okami]] and [[Toyozawa]]. The [[Katsuran Southern Line]], a conventional rail service, runs along the western coast connecting the interior towns.

=== Road ===
The [[Tachibara Expressway]] is the primary road link, connecting the prefectural capital to the [[Ishijima Coastal Expressway]] at the Ehime border. The [[Udon Road]], a scenic route that follows the Tachibara Udon Road through the interior valleys, is one of the most popular driving routes in Okaiken for food tourists.

== Education ==

The [[Tachibara Fermentation Institute]] is the prefecture's premier research institution, specialising in the science of traditional fermentation processes, food microbiology, and natural dye chemistry. The [[Tachibara Udon Academy]], based in [[Kawakami, Tachibara|Kawakami]], trains noodle makers in the traditional foot-kneading technique and operates a certification programme for Tachibara udon producers.

== Notable people ==

* [[Kawakami Shiori]] (born 1970) — current Governor of Tachibara Prefecture, former president of the Kawakami Shōyu brewery
* [[Kawakami Nobumitsu]] (1842–1910) — last ''daimyō'' of the Tachibara Domain, first Governor of Tachibara Prefecture
* [[Oshika Yuzo]] (1932–2018) — master indigo dyer, leader of the Tachibara Indigo Revival
* [[Kitanada Hana]] (born 1986) — food scientist, developer of the Tachibara Udon Preservation Method for long-distance shipping

